<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="basics-of-voting">
    <title>Basics of Voting</title>

    <outcomes>
        <ol marker="1)">
            <li>
                <p>Interpret individual preference ballots and preference schedules.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>Determine the number of voters represented by a preference schedule.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>Determine whether a choice has a majority of the first-place votes.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>Use pairwise comparisons to identify a Condorcet winner, when one exists.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>Explain and apply the majority, Condorcet, monotonicity, and independence of irrelevant alternatives criteria.</p>
            </li>
        </ol>
    </outcomes>

    <subsection xml:id="preference-ballots-schedules">
        <title>Preference Ballots and Preference Schedules</title>

        <p>
            Many group decisions involve more than two possible choices. A ballot that records
            only a voter's favorite choice does not show how that voter feels about the remaining
            choices. Preference ballots preserve this additional information.
        </p>

        <definition xml:id="def-preference-ballot">
            <title>Preference Ballot</title>
            <statement>
                <p>

                </p>
            </statement>
        </definition>

        <definition xml:id="def-preference-schedule">
            <title>Preference Schedule</title>
            <statement>
                <p>

                </p>
            </statement>
        </definition>

        <example xml:id="ex-build-preference-schedule">
            <title>Redesigning a Campus Courtyard</title>

            <p>
                Eight students rank three possible additions to a campus courtyard: a reading
                garden (R), a game area (G), and a bicycle station (B). Their individual
                preference ballots are shown below.
            </p>

            <table xml:id="table-courtyard-individual-ballots">
                <title>Individual Ballots for the Courtyard Redesign</title>
                <tabular row-headers="yes"
                         top="minor"
                         bottom="minor"
                         left="minor"
                         right="minor"
                         halign="center">
                    <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
                        <cell>Voter</cell>
                        <cell>1</cell>
                        <cell>2</cell>
                        <cell>3</cell>
                        <cell>4</cell>
                        <cell>5</cell>
                        <cell>6</cell>
                        <cell>7</cell>
                        <cell>8</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>First choice</cell>
                        <cell>R</cell>
                        <cell>R</cell>
                        <cell>G</cell>
                        <cell>G</cell>
                        <cell>B</cell>
                        <cell>R</cell>
                        <cell>B</cell>
                        <cell>G</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>Second choice</cell>
                        <cell>G</cell>
                        <cell>G</cell>
                        <cell>R</cell>
                        <cell>B</cell>
                        <cell>G</cell>
                        <cell>B</cell>
                        <cell>G</cell>
                        <cell>R</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>Third choice</cell>
                        <cell>B</cell>
                        <cell>B</cell>
                        <cell>B</cell>
                        <cell>R</cell>
                        <cell>R</cell>
                        <cell>G</cell>
                        <cell>R</cell>
                        <cell>B</cell>
                    </row>
                </tabular>
            </table>

            <ol marker="1)">
                <li>
                    <p>How many different preference orders appear among the eight ballots?</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>Group identical ballots together and construct a preference schedule.</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>How many students prefer the game area to the bicycle station?</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>How many students rank the reading garden last?</p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="ex-read-preference-schedule">
            <title>Nature Center Weekend Program</title>

            <p>
                Volunteers at a nature center rank three possible weekend programs: bird
                banding (B), a pond survey (P), and tree identification (T).
            </p>

            <table xml:id="table-nature-center-program">
                <title>Preference Schedule for the Weekend Program</title>
                <tabular row-headers="yes"
                         top="minor"
                         bottom="minor"
                         left="minor"
                         right="minor"
                         halign="center">
                    <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
                        <cell>Number of voters</cell>
                        <cell>7</cell>
                        <cell>5</cell>
                        <cell>4</cell>
                        <cell>3</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>First choice</cell>
                        <cell>B</cell>
                        <cell>P</cell>
                        <cell>T</cell>
                        <cell>B</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>Second choice</cell>
                        <cell>P</cell>
                        <cell>T</cell>
                        <cell>P</cell>
                        <cell>T</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>Third choice</cell>
                        <cell>T</cell>
                        <cell>B</cell>
                        <cell>B</cell>
                        <cell>P</cell>
                    </row>
                </tabular>
            </table>

            <ol marker="1)">
                <li>
                    <p>How many volunteers voted?</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>How many volunteers selected bird banding as their first choice?</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>How many volunteers prefer the pond survey to tree identification?</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>How many volunteers prefer tree identification to bird banding?</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>Describe the complete preference order of the four voters represented by the third column.</p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </example>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="majority-voting-basics">
        <title>Majority</title>

        <definition xml:id="def-majority">
            <title>Majority</title>
            <statement>
                <p>

                </p>
            </statement>
        </definition>

        <p>
            A choice can receive more first-place votes than every other choice without
            receiving a majority. To have a majority, the choice must receive more than half
            of all first-place votes.
        </p>

        <example xml:id="ex-most-votes-no-majority">
            <title>Choosing a Community Garden Plan</title>

            <p>
                A neighborhood association is choosing among three plans for a community
                garden: a vegetable garden (V), a native-flower garden (N), and a mixed-use
                garden (M). The first-place vote totals are shown below.
            </p>

            <table xml:id="table-community-garden-first-place">
                <title>First-Place Votes for the Community Garden</title>
                <tabular row-headers="yes"
                         top="minor"
                         bottom="minor"
                         left="minor"
                         right="minor"
                         halign="center">
                    <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
                        <cell>Plan</cell>
                        <cell>V</cell>
                        <cell>N</cell>
                        <cell>M</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>First-place votes</cell>
                        <cell>9</cell>
                        <cell>6</cell>
                        <cell>4</cell>
                    </row>
                </tabular>
            </table>

            <ol marker="1)">
                <li>
                    <p>How many people voted?</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>What is the smallest number of votes needed for a majority?</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>Does any plan have a majority? Explain.</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>How many additional first-place votes would Plan V need to have a majority if the total number of voters remained unchanged?</p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="ex-identify-majority-winner">
            <title>Selecting an Animal Shelter Logo</title>

            <p>
                Twenty-four volunteers vote on three possible animal shelter logos: a dog
                silhouette (D), a cat silhouette (C), and a paw print (P). Thirteen volunteers
                rank D first, seven rank C first, and four rank P first.
            </p>

            <ol marker="1)">
                <li>
                    <p>What is the smallest number of first-place votes needed for a majority?</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>Is there a majority winner? If so, identify it.</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>What percentage of the volunteers ranked the majority winner first? Round to one decimal place.</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>If one voter changed from D to C, would there still be a majority winner? Explain.</p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </example>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="pairwise-comparisons-condorcet">
        <title>Pairwise Comparisons and Condorcet Winners</title>

        <definition xml:id="def-pairwise-comparison">
            <title>Pairwise Comparison</title>
            <statement>
                <p>

                </p>
            </statement>
        </definition>

        <definition xml:id="def-condorcet-winner">
            <title>Condorcet Winner</title>
            <statement>
                <p>

                </p>
            </statement>
        </definition>

        <p>
            In a pairwise comparison, imagine that only two choices remain. Each voter supports
            whichever of those two choices appears higher on that voter's preference ballot.
            A choice does not need to be ranked first on a ballot to receive that voter's support
            in a particular pairwise comparison.
        </p>

        <example xml:id="ex-find-condorcet-winner">
            <title>Naming a Nature Preserve Trail</title>

            <p>
                A committee ranks three possible names for a new nature preserve trail: Blue
                Heron (H), Maple Ridge (M), and River Bend (R).
            </p>

            <table xml:id="table-trail-name-preferences">
                <title>Preference Schedule for the Trail Name</title>
                <tabular row-headers="yes"
                         top="minor"
                         bottom="minor"
                         left="minor"
                         right="minor"
                         halign="center">
                    <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
                        <cell>Number of voters</cell>
                        <cell>6</cell>
                        <cell>5</cell>
                        <cell>4</cell>
                        <cell>2</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>First choice</cell>
                        <cell>H</cell>
                        <cell>M</cell>
                        <cell>R</cell>
                        <cell>M</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>Second choice</cell>
                        <cell>M</cell>
                        <cell>R</cell>
                        <cell>M</cell>
                        <cell>H</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>Third choice</cell>
                        <cell>R</cell>
                        <cell>H</cell>
                        <cell>H</cell>
                        <cell>R</cell>
                    </row>
                </tabular>
            </table>

            <ol marker="1)">
                <li>
                    <p>In a pairwise comparison between H and M, how many voters prefer each choice?</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>In a pairwise comparison between H and R, how many voters prefer each choice?</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>In a pairwise comparison between M and R, how many voters prefer each choice?</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>Is there a Condorcet winner? If so, identify it and explain why it qualifies.</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>Does the Condorcet winner also have a majority of the first-place votes?</p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="ex-condorcet-cycle">
            <title>Selecting a Field-Research Project</title>

            <p>
                Students rank three possible field-research projects: a wetland survey (W), a
                forest survey (F), and a river survey (R).
            </p>

            <table xml:id="table-field-research-preferences">
                <title>Preference Schedule for the Field-Research Project</title>
                <tabular row-headers="yes"
                         top="minor"
                         bottom="minor"
                         left="minor"
                         right="minor"
                         halign="center">
                    <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
                        <cell>Number of voters</cell>
                        <cell>7</cell>
                        <cell>5</cell>
                        <cell>4</cell>
                        <cell>3</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>First choice</cell>
                        <cell>W</cell>
                        <cell>F</cell>
                        <cell>R</cell>
                        <cell>R</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>Second choice</cell>
                        <cell>F</cell>
                        <cell>R</cell>
                        <cell>F</cell>
                        <cell>W</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>Third choice</cell>
                        <cell>R</cell>
                        <cell>W</cell>
                        <cell>W</cell>
                        <cell>F</cell>
                    </row>
                </tabular>
            </table>

            <ol marker="1)">
                <li>
                    <p>Which project is preferred in the pairwise comparison between W and F?</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>Which project is preferred in the pairwise comparison between F and R?</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>Which project is preferred in the pairwise comparison between R and W?</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>Does this election have a Condorcet winner? Explain.</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>Describe the cycle formed by the three pairwise results.</p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </example>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="fairness-criteria">
        <title>Fairness Criteria</title>

        <p>
            A fairness criterion is a property that we might reasonably want a voting method
            to satisfy. Later, these criteria can be used to evaluate the strengths and
            limitations of different voting methods.
        </p>

        <definition xml:id="def-fairness-criterion">
            <title>Fairness Criterion</title>
            <statement>
                <p>

                </p>
            </statement>
        </definition>

        <definition xml:id="def-majority-criterion">
            <title>Majority Criterion</title>
            <statement>
                <p>

                </p>
            </statement>
        </definition>

        <definition xml:id="def-condorcet-criterion">
            <title>Condorcet Criterion</title>
            <statement>
                <p>

                </p>
            </statement>
        </definition>

        <definition xml:id="def-monotonicity-criterion">
            <title>Monotonicity Criterion</title>
            <statement>
                <p>

                </p>
            </statement>
        </definition>

        <definition xml:id="def-iia-criterion">
            <title>Independence of Irrelevant Alternatives Criterion</title>
            <statement>
                <p>

                </p>
            </statement>
        </definition>

        <example xml:id="ex-match-fairness-criteria">
            <title>Matching Situations to Fairness Criteria</title>

            <p>
                For each situation, identify the fairness criterion that has been violated and
                explain your reasoning.
            </p>

            <ol marker="1)">
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Of 31 voters, 18 rank choice A first. The voting method declares choice
                        B the winner.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Choice C defeats each of choices A, B, and D in separate head-to-head
                        comparisons, but the voting method declares A the winner.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Choice E initially wins. On a second ballot, several voters move E
                        higher in their rankings and make no other changes. Under the same
                        method, E now loses.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Choice G wins an election. A non-winning choice is removed, and the
                        relative rankings of every remaining pair of choices stay unchanged.
                        When the votes are recounted, choice H wins.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="ex-analyze-majority-condorcet-criteria">
            <title>Two Criteria Violated at Once</title>

            <p>
                In an election with 27 voters, choice K receives 15 first-place votes. In
                addition, K defeats each of the other choices in a pairwise comparison. The
                voting method declares choice M the winner.
            </p>

            <ol marker="1)">
                <li>
                    <p>Does K have a majority of the first-place votes? Show how you know.</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>Is K a Condorcet winner? Explain.</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>Which fairness criteria are violated by this result?</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Can you determine from the information given whether monotonicity or
                        independence of irrelevant alternatives was violated? Explain.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="ex-monotonicity-revote">
            <title>Reconsidering a Campus Mural</title>

            <p>
                A committee uses its established voting method to select a design for a campus
                mural. Design L wins. Before the result is finalized, eight committee members
                submit corrected ballots. Each of these voters moves L from second place to
                first place while keeping the relative order of all other designs unchanged.
                When the same voting method is applied to the corrected ballots, design N wins.
            </p>

            <ol marker="1)">
                <li>
                    <p>How did the corrected ballots change the support for L?</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>Which fairness criterion is violated?</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>Explain why the change in winner conflicts with that criterion.</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>Would the criterion necessarily be violated if L had remained the winner? Explain.</p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="ex-iia-science-fair-theme">
            <title>Selecting a Science-Fair Theme</title>

            <p>
                A school uses a voting method to choose among four science-fair themes:
                ecosystems (E), oceans (O), space (S), and technology (T). Ecosystems wins the
                original election, and technology finishes last. The school later discovers
                that the technology theme is unavailable and removes it. No voter changes the
                relative order of ecosystems, oceans, and space. When the same voting method is
                applied to the remaining choices, oceans wins.
            </p>

            <ol marker="1)">
                <li>
                    <p>Why is technology an irrelevant alternative when comparing the original and revised results?</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>Which fairness criterion is violated?</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>What would have needed to happen for the voting method to satisfy this criterion?</p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </example>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="basics-voting-mixed-examples">
        <title>Additional Examples</title>

        <example xml:id="ex-wildlife-center-exhibit">
            <title>Choosing a Wildlife-Center Exhibit</title>

            <p>
                A wildlife center asks its advisory group to rank three possible new exhibits:
                animal tracking (A), wetland ecology (W), and pollinator conservation (P).
            </p>

            <table xml:id="table-wildlife-exhibit-preferences">
                <title>Preference Schedule for the Wildlife-Center Exhibit</title>
                <tabular row-headers="yes"
                         top="minor"
                         bottom="minor"
                         left="minor"
                         right="minor"
                         halign="center">
                    <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
                        <cell>Number of voters</cell>
                        <cell>8</cell>
                        <cell>6</cell>
                        <cell>5</cell>
                        <cell>4</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>First choice</cell>
                        <cell>A</cell>
                        <cell>W</cell>
                        <cell>P</cell>
                        <cell>W</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>Second choice</cell>
                        <cell>W</cell>
                        <cell>P</cell>
                        <cell>W</cell>
                        <cell>A</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>Third choice</cell>
                        <cell>P</cell>
                        <cell>A</cell>
                        <cell>A</cell>
                        <cell>P</cell>
                    </row>
                </tabular>
            </table>

            <ol marker="1)">
                <li>
                    <p>How many advisory-group members voted?</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>How many first-place votes are required for a majority?</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>Does any exhibit have a majority of the first-place votes?</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>Determine the winner of each pairwise comparison.</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>Is there a Condorcet winner? Explain.</p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="ex-majority-implies-condorcet">
            <title>Connecting Majority and Pairwise Comparisons</title>

            <p>
                Suppose choice Q is ranked first by 26 of 49 voters in an election. The other
                23 voters may rank Q anywhere on their ballots.
            </p>

            <ol marker="1)">
                <li>
                    <p>Does Q have a majority of the first-place votes?</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        In a pairwise comparison between Q and any other choice, how many voters
                        are guaranteed to prefer Q?
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>What is the greatest possible number of voters who could prefer the other choice to Q?</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>Explain why every majority winner must also be a Condorcet winner.</p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </example>
    </subsection>
</section>
